Below is the corrected senior developer interview cheatsheet with a simple code example, input, output, key difference, and interview one-liner for each topic.

==============================================================================
TOP 13 SENIOR DEVELOPER STREAM DIFFERENCE QUESTIONS WITH CODE
==============================================================================

1. map() vs flatMap()
==============================================================================

map()
-----
Transforms each element into one new element.

Input:
["Java", "Spring"]

Code:

List<Integer> lengths =
    List.of("Java", "Spring")
        .stream()
        .map(String::length)
        .toList();

Output:
[4, 6]


flatMap()
---------
Transforms and flattens nested data into a single Stream.

Input:
[[1,2], [3,4]]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(List.of(1,2), List.of(3,4))
        .stream()
        .flatMap(List::stream)
        .toList();

Output:
[1, 2, 3, 4]

Difference:
map()     = Transform
flatMap() = Transform + Flatten

Interview One-Liner:
Use map() for one-to-one transformation and flatMap() for
one-to-many transformation or nested collections.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. reduce() vs collect()
==============================================================================

reduce()
--------
Combines all elements into one value.

Input:
[10, 20, 30]

Code:

int sum =
    List.of(10,20,30)
        .stream()
        .reduce(0, Integer::sum);

Output:
60


collect()
---------
Collects elements into a mutable result container.

Input:
[10, 20, 30]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(10,20,30)
        .stream()
        .collect(Collectors.toList());

Output:
[10, 20, 30]

Difference:
reduce()  = Many elements to one immutable value
collect() = Many elements to a collection or mutable result

Interview One-Liner:
Use reduce() for aggregation and collect() for List, Set, Map,
grouping, partitioning, or mutable reduction.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

3. findFirst() vs findAny()
==============================================================================

findFirst()
-----------
Returns the first element according to encounter order.

Input:
[10, 20, 30]

Code:

Integer first =
    List.of(10,20,30)
        .stream()
        .findFirst()
        .orElse(null);

Output:
10


findAny()
---------
Returns any available element.

Input:
[10, 20, 30]

Code:

Integer any =
    List.of(10,20,30)
        .parallelStream()
        .findAny()
        .orElse(null);

Possible Output:
10
or 20
or 30

Difference:
findFirst() = Preserves encounter order
findAny()   = Any available element

Interview One-Liner:
Prefer findAny() when encounter order is unimportant, especially
in a parallel Stream.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

4. stream() vs parallelStream()
==============================================================================

stream()
--------
Processes elements sequentially.

Input:
[1, 2, 3, 4]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4)
        .stream()
        .map(n -> n * 2)
        .toList();

Output:
[2, 4, 6, 8]


parallelStream()
----------------
Processes elements using parallel execution.

Input:
[1, 2, 3, 4]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4)
        .parallelStream()
        .map(n -> n * 2)
        .toList();

Output:
[2, 4, 6, 8]

Difference:
stream()         = Sequential execution
parallelStream() = Parallel execution using ForkJoinPool

Important:
toList() preserves encounter order for this ordered source, but
forEach() execution order is not guaranteed in a parallel Stream.

Interview One-Liner:
Use parallelStream() only after measuring performance for a large,
CPU-intensive, stateless workload.

