9. limit() vs skip()
==============================================================================

limit()
-------
Keeps the first N elements.

Input:
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4,5)
        .stream()
        .limit(3)
        .toList();

Output:
[1, 2, 3]


skip()
------
Ignores the first N elements.

Input:
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4,5)
        .stream()
        .skip(3)
        .toList();

Output:
[4, 5]

Difference:
limit(n) = Take first N
skip(n)  = Ignore first N

Combined Pagination Example:

int pageNumber = 2;
int pageSize = 2;

List<Integer> page =
    List.of(10,20,30,40,50)
        .stream()
        .skip((long)(pageNumber - 1) * pageSize)
        .limit(pageSize)
        .toList();

Output:
[30, 40]

Interview One-Liner:
Use skip() and limit() together for in-memory pagination, but not as
a replacement for database-level pagination on large datasets.

