10. sorted() vs sorted(Comparator)
==============================================================================

sorted()
--------
Uses natural ordering.

Input:
[30, 10, 20]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(30,10,20)
        .stream()
        .sorted()
        .toList();

Output:
[10, 20, 30]


sorted(Comparator)
------------------
Uses custom ordering.

Input:
[30, 10, 20]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(30,10,20)
        .stream()
        .sorted(Comparator.reverseOrder())
        .toList();

Output:
[30, 20, 10]

Employee Example:

List<Employee> result =
    employees.stream()
             .sorted(
                 Comparator.comparing(Employee::getSalary)
                           .reversed()
                           .thenComparing(Employee::getName))
             .toList();

Difference:
sorted()           = Natural order
sorted(Comparator) = Custom or multi-field order

Interview One-Liner:
Use sorted() for natural ordering and provide a Comparator for
reverse, property-based, or multi-level sorting.

