11. Optional vs Null
==============================================================================

Null
----
Requires explicit checking.

Code:

String name = null;

if(name != null) {
    System.out.println(name.toUpperCase());
}

Output:
No Output


Optional
--------
Represents presence or absence explicitly.

Code:

String result =
    Optional.ofNullable(null)
            .map(String::toUpperCase)
            .orElse("UNKNOWN");

Output:
UNKNOWN

Difference:
null     = Absence is implicit
Optional = Absence is explicit

Interview One-Liner:
Optional makes absence explicit and supports fluent transformations,
but it is mainly intended for return types rather than every field
or method parameter.

