12. Lazy Evaluation vs Eager Evaluation
==============================================================================

Lazy Evaluation
---------------
Intermediate Stream operations execute only when a terminal operation runs.

Input:
[1, 2, 3]

Code:

Stream<Integer> stream =
    List.of(1,2,3)
        .stream()
        .filter(n -> {
            System.out.println("Checking " + n);
            return n > 1;
        });

Output:
No Output

Reason:
No terminal operation has been called.


After Terminal Operation:

long count = stream.count();

Output:
Checking 1
Checking 2
Checking 3

Result:
2


Eager Evaluation
----------------
The operation executes immediately.

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    new ArrayList<>();

for(Integer n : List.of(1,2,3)) {
    if(n > 1) {
        result.add(n);
    }
}

Output:
[2, 3]

Difference:
Lazy  = Execute only when required
Eager = Execute immediately

Interview One-Liner:
Lazy evaluation allows operation fusion and short-circuiting because
the Stream delays processing until a terminal operation runs.

