13. Stateful vs Stateless Operations
==============================================================================

Stateless Operation
-------------------
Processes each element independently.

Examples:
filter()
map()
flatMap()
peek()

Input:
[1, 2, 3, 4]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(1,2,3,4)
        .stream()
        .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
        .map(n -> n * 10)
        .toList();

Output:
[20, 40]


Stateful Operation
------------------
Requires information about other elements.

Examples:
sorted()
distinct()

Input:
[3, 1, 2, 2]

Code:

List<Integer> result =
    List.of(3,1,2,2)
        .stream()
        .distinct()
        .sorted()
        .toList();

Output:
[1, 2, 3]

Difference:
Stateless = Each element is independent
Stateful  = Maintains information across elements

Important Correction:
limit() and skip() are stateful short-circuiting intermediate operations.
Their performance cost is especially noticeable on ordered parallel Streams.

Interview One-Liner:
Stateless operations are easier to parallelize, while stateful operations
may require buffering, coordination, or knowledge of previously seen elements.

==============================================================================
TOP 5 SENIOR DEVELOPER MUST-PREPARE DIFFERENCES
==============================================================================

1. map() vs flatMap()
   map transforms; flatMap transforms and flattens.

2. reduce() vs collect()
   reduce aggregates to one value; collect gathers into a container.

3. stream() vs parallelStream()
   Sequential vs parallel, but parallel must be benchmarked.

4. groupingBy() vs partitioningBy()
   Multiple categories vs two Boolean groups.

5. Stateful vs Stateless
   Cross-element state vs independent element processing.

